<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>
    <data noupdate="1">
        <!-- Read this before 3.01, it explains why these records look the way they
             do. They used to set loan_amount directly. That works while loan_amount
             is a plain column, but the moment you make it computed at 3.01 a direct
             write runs your inverse method instead: it works a down_payment back out
             of a principal_amount nobody set, and the 2500.00 below is replaced by a
             negative figure. The CHECK constraint you add at 3.02 then refuses the
             record outright, because its principal is zero.

             So they set principal_amount and down_payment, and let loan_amount fall
             out of the arithmetic. That is the general lesson: once a field is
             derived, writing to it is no longer the same thing as setting it.

             Until you do 3.01 these applications show a loan amount of zero, since
             nothing sets it and nothing works it out yet. Making that column right
             is the first thing 3.01 does. -->
        <record id="loan_application_demo_1" model="loan.application">
            <field name="name">LOAN/2026/001</field>
            <field name="partner_id" ref="base.res_partner_1"/>
            <field name="product_id" ref="product.product_product_4"/>
            <field name="date_applied">2026-01-15</field>
            <field name="principal_amount">15000.00</field>
            <field name="down_payment">2500.00</field>
            <field name="loan_term">48</field>
            <field name="interest_rate">7.50</field>
            <field name="state">sent</field>
        </record>
        <record id="loan_application_demo_2" model="loan.application">
            <field name="name">LOAN/2026/002</field>
            <field name="partner_id" ref="base.res_partner_2"/>
            <field name="product_id" ref="product.product_product_3"/>
            <field name="date_applied" eval="(DateTime.now() - relativedelta(months=5)).strftime('%Y-%m-%d')"/>
            <field name="principal_amount">20000.00</field>
            <field name="down_payment">2500.00</field>
            <field name="loan_term">48</field>
            <field name="interest_rate">7.50</field>
            <field name="state">draft</field>
        </record>
        <record id="loan_application_demo_3" model="loan.application">
            <field name="name">LOAN/2026/003</field>
            <field name="partner_id" ref="base.res_partner_3"/>
            <field name="date_applied" eval="(DateTime.now() - relativedelta(months=2)).strftime('%Y-%m-%d')"/>
            <field name="principal_amount">20000.00</field>
            <field name="down_payment">2500.00</field>
            <field name="loan_term">48</field>
            <field name="interest_rate">7.50</field>
            <field name="state">draft</field>
        </record>

        <!-- TODO (3.03): these records set state by hand, which walks straight
             past the workflow you just built. Leave them for now: putting them
             through the real methods means every application needs its compliance
             documents first, and those do not exist yet. You come back to this at
             3.04, once create() builds the checklist for you. -->

        <!-- OPTIONAL (3.04): a stretch exercise, not part of the chapter. Skip it if
             you are short of time.

             Now that create() fills in the checklist, these records get their
             document lines for free, and the state above can stop being a
             hand-written lie. Replace it with the methods that earn it:

                 <function model="loan.application.document" name="action_approve_document"
                          eval="[obj().search([('application_id','=',ref('loan_application_demo_1')),
                                               ('type_id.is_required','=',True)]).ids]"/>
                 <function model="loan.application" name="action_submit"
                          eval="[[ref('loan_application_demo_1')]]"/>

             Approve first, then submit: action_submit refuses an application whose
             required documents are not settled, which is the whole point of it.
             Drop the state field from loan_application_demo_1 and let the calls set
             it. Odoo's own demo data works this way, e.g. crm_lead_demo.xml calling
             action_set_won. Only demo_1 needs it, since draft is the default.

             Two details that decide whether this works:

             1. `obj` in an eval is that function's own model, so obj().search(...)
                searches loan.application.document. The lines are created by your
                override and have no xml ids, so searching is how you reach them.
             2. A function node runs in file order, so it has to sit after the
                records it acts on. Inside data noupdate="1" it runs on install only
                and is skipped on a module update.

             Note that action_submit stamps date_applied with today, so demo_1 gives
             up its fixed 2026-01-15 date.

             Worth knowing before you spend time on it: demo data is loaded when the
             module is installed, not when it is updated, and a function node inside
             noupdate="1" is skipped on an update entirely. On a build that only
             updates the module you will write this correctly and see nothing change.
             It takes a fresh install to observe. -->

    </data>
</odoo>
